Received: " Every control of this library (`ocelle-button`, and the action row, navigation items, copy control and form controls after it) follows these rules. 1. **One inner control.** Each control renders exactly one native `<button type="button">` (button mode) or `<a>` (link mode) in an open shadow root attached with `delegatesFocus`; the host has no role and no tabindex. `element.focus()` focuses the inner control, and `element.click()` clicks it, so a click started by `click()` gets the same guarantees as any other click. A click dispatched on the host with `dispatchEvent` is outside the guarantees: it does not pass through the inner control, so the element stops it. The host is a grid container (`inline-grid` for `ocelle-button`) and the inner control its single stretched item, so the inner control fills the host when a flex row or a grid stretches it; a stretched button grows to the row's height (set `align-self` on the element to avoid it). A host may restyle the element's `display` and size: the element also listens for `click` and `auxclick` on the host from its constructor and stops any that did not pass through the inner control, in every state, so host area outside the inner control never activates it. Capture-phase listeners, and listeners added to the element before it is upgraded, are outside that guarantee: they run before the element's own listener. 2. **Link versus button.** A control is in link mode exactly when its `href` is not empty or whitespace only, parses as a URL against the document's base URL and is not a `javascript:` URL as the URL parser reads it (so `JAVASCRIPT:`, `java\tscript:` and a leading space or control character count); otherwise it is in button mode, with no error for an `href` that does not parse. Link mode renders an `<a>` with the `href` as written and the native link behaviour: Enter activates, Space does not, and modifier clicks get the browser's own modifier handling (the element prevents nothing); button mode renders a `<button type="button">` (Enter and Space activate). Both modes look the same for the same variant. `data:` and `vbscript:` URLs are link mode and left to the host: Chromium blocks a top-level `data:` navigation and runs no `vbscript:`. 3. **Disabled.** `disabled` means the control cannot be used here at all: the inner control is out of the tab order, reported disabled and not activated. Not activated means: no `ocelle-*` event and no `click` reaching a bubble-phase listener on the element or its ancestors (up to `document` and `window`); capture-phase listeners are outside it. A disabled link has no `href`: it is an `<a role="link" aria-disabled="true">` out of the tab order, so neither a click, a modifier click, a middle click, the context menu nor a drag opens it. **Unavailable.** Unavailable means the control exists but cannot be used right now: the inner control stays in the tab order and keeps focus, reports `aria-disabled="true"`, keeps its name and description so a user can learn why, and is not activated. An unavailable link has no `href` either: it is an `<a role="link" tabindex="0" aria-disabled="true">` that stays focusable, so neither a click, a modifier click, a middle click, Enter, the context menu nor a drag opens it. A busy `ocelle-button` is unavailable; `disabled` wins over it. 4. **Host ARIA is moved.** The ARIA attributes a control lists, and `title`, are moved from the host onto the inner control: the element applies the value there and removes the attribute from the host, so the state appears once, on the inner control, and `getAttribute` on the host returns `null` afterwards. To clear a moved state, set an empty or whitespace-only value (`aria-controls=""`, `aria-label=""`, `title=""`) or the ARIA off value (`aria-expanded="false"`, `aria-pressed="false"`, `aria-current="false"`); any other value is applied as written. `removeAttribute` on a moved attribute changes nothing, and neither does a framework binding that becomes `undefined`, `null` or `nothing`, so bind the off value: | Stack | Bind | Never | |---|---|---| | React | `aria-current={active ? "page" : "false"}`, `aria-expanded={open ? "true" : "false"}`, `aria-controls={open ? "panel" : ""}` | `undefined` or `null` | | Lit | `aria-current=${active ? "page" : "false"}` (an attribute binding with a string) | `nothing`, `ifDefined(undefined)` | | Svelte | `aria-current={active ? "page" : "false"}` | `undefined`, `null` | | htmx, Thymeleaf | render the attribute with the off value (`th:attr="aria-current=${active} ? 'page' : 'false'"`) | omitting the attribute on an element that is updated in place; writing a `data-ocelle-moved-*` attribute | Each moved value is mirrored in a host attribute the element owns, `data-ocelle-moved-<attribute>` (for example `data-ocelle-moved-aria-label`), removed when the value is cleared; at upgrade the element reads a mirror back for an attribute the host does not set, and a host attribute set at upgrade wins over its mirror. So copies of the live DOM (`cloneNode(true)`, `innerHTML` and `outerHTML`, htmx's history snapshot) keep names and states. The element restores a mirror that is removed or changed while it holds the value, so a morphing swap (htmx's idiomorph `morph` swap, Turbo 8 page-refresh morphs), which removes every attribute the server markup lacks, keeps the mirrors. Hosts never write or remove a mirror: one written while the element holds no value for it is not read until the next upgrade. With htmx, a swap that replaces the element moves the new element's attributes at upgrade; a morphing swap sets the changed attributes on the live element, which moves them, and the element restores the mirrors the morph removes; htmx's history restore keeps the moved values through the mirrors. Since a host attribute selector no longer matches a moved state, `ocelle-button` exposes custom states: `:state(expanded)` while the moved `aria-expanded` is `"true"`, `:state(pressed)` while the moved `aria-pressed` is `"t